# The export output. ~56 GB — it is a build product, never committed. A fresh clone re-runs the
# export; `--incremental` (the decompiler's own --vpk_cache) makes that far cheaper than the first
# run. Point a consumer's build at it with CS2_EXPORT_OUT; the side generators write into it too.
out/
out-sample/

# The decompiler and any other downloaded tool binaries.
.tools/

# ShaderDump is a C# helper; these are its build products. bin/ alone is ~1.1 GB.
ShaderDump/bin/
ShaderDump/obj/


# The run logs — one file per invocation, the newest 10 kept, written line by line as the run goes.
# NOT committed, for the same reason as .env: THE REPO IS PUBLIC, and a log carries absolute paths,
# the machine's OS build, where CS2 is installed and whatever a subprocess chose to print. Secrets
# are scrubbed by value before anything is written (runlog.ts), but that is the second line of
# defence and this is the first.
logs/

# Run logs and diagnostics. Every one of these is regenerated by the run that names it, and the
# real copies are written into out/data/ — the root-level ones were leftovers from an older layout.
*-log.txt
link-report.txt
shader-index.txt
exceptions.txt

# run.bat hands its arguments to its own elevated relaunch through a file rather than through the
# command line, because quotes do not survive batch -> PowerShell -> cmd. The elevated copy deletes
# it on read; one can only be left behind if UAC was declined mid-flight.
.run-args-*.tmp

node_modules/
.DS_Store
*.tsbuildinfo

# Credentials. THE REPO IS PUBLIC — an R2 secret committed here is a secret published to the world,
# and rotating it is the only remedy once it has been pushed. publish.ts reads these from the
# environment and never prints them; keep them in a file git cannot see.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
